<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Swaziland Protectorate</h1><p class="st-meta">1903 CE – 1968 CE · capital Mbabane</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The British protectorate over the Swazi kingdom, during which King Sobhuza II fought to recover land lost to concessions.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic</a> had administered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swaziland</a> from 1894. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> took over in 1903 and placed it under the High Commissioner for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">South Africa</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbabane">Mbabane</a> as the administrative centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1907 a partition of land left only about a third for Swazi use, the rest going to concession holders and settlers. The queen regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labotsibeni-mdluli">Labotsibeni</a> led opposition until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobhuza-ii">Sobhuza II</a> came of age in 1921; he petitioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judicial-committee-of-the-privy-council">Privy Council</a> and later bought back land through a national fund. Mining of asbestos and iron and forestry grew after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swaziland</a> became independent on 6 September 1968 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobhuza-ii">Sobhuza II</a> as king, part of the continuity of the Swazi Kingdom. He abolished the constitution in 1973, and the country was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a> in 2018.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/swaziland-protectorate.jpg" alt="Swaziland Protectorate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sobhuza II · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Eswatini.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1903 CE – 1968 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mbabane</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Labotsibeni, Sobhuza II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, Swazi traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>siSwati, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/eswatini">Eswatini</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Swaziland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=swaziland-protectorate" aria-label="Swaziland Protectorate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5502 3144 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Swaziland Protectorate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5822 3400L5823 3405M5822 3400L4997 651M5822 3400L5746 3380"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5823 3405h0M4997 651h0M5746 3380h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5822 3400h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5822 3400h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mbabane. <a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=swaziland-protectorate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

